# Mistral Managed Agents

Build an agent in Python. Try it on your laptop, then run the same code in production with one command.

An agent is a model and its instructions, plus the tools, sandboxes, connectors, skills and subagents it can use. The same agent runs in your process, on your own server with `agents serve`, or on Mistral with `agents deploy`.

## Install

You need Python 3.12, 3.13 or 3.14, and a Mistral API key from [Studio](https://console.mistral.ai/api-keys).

```bash
uv init --python 3.12 my_agent && cd my_agent
uv add mistralai-agents
export MISTRAL_API_KEY="<your-key>"
```

With pip: `pip install mistralai-agents`.

## Hello world

```python
import asyncio

from mistralai import agents

agent = agents.Agent(model="mistral-large-4")


async def main() -> None:
    result = await agent.run("Say hello")
    print(result.text)


asyncio.run(main())
```

`agent.run` sends one message and returns the result when the agent has answered.

## Create a project

`agents init` creates a project with an agent, a tool and a Dockerfile. Chat with the agent in your terminal. Then push the project to a GitHub repository and deploy it:

```bash
uvx --python 3.12 --from mistralai-agents agents init hello-agent
cd hello-agent
uv run --env-file .env agents chat
git remote add origin <repository-url>
git push -u origin HEAD
uv run --env-file .env agents deploy
```

These git commands assume `agents init` created the repository and committed the project. If it did not, the `Next steps` panel it prints has the commands for your case.

